#N canvas 20 20 2600 2100 12;
#X text 20 20 c_mapped -- one destination's scaling and its row on the screen. It scales u_map's looked-up value into the destination's units \, passes it on while live \, and draws the row: the value while live \, the latched value and the knob's position while pickup holds it. One per destination that carries a value \, so the scaling lives with its destination., f 84;
#X text 20 220 creation arg 1: the ROW NAME as a makefilename format -- bpm \, or volca-cc%d where %d takes the map row's arg. REQUIRED. creation arg 2: the multiplier \, 127 for a MIDI value \, 210 for tempo. creation arg 3: the offset added after it \, 0 or 40. inlet 1: a list of four floats -- arg \, value 0 to 1 \, target 0 to 1 \, live 1 or 0. outlet 1: arg then the scaled value \, as a list \, ONLY WHILE LIVE., f 84;
#X text 20 400 The action is gated on the assembled message: while pickup holds a knob the handler hears nothing at all. The row still draws (item 242)., f 84;
#X text 20 560 The held row is name <latched> (<knob>) \, as in bpm 57 (120). The value must be a float for g_oled's makefilename %g \, so the second number rides in the unit field. The row lives here and not in u_map's pickup because only this knows what the knob maps to (item 240)., f 84;
#X text 20 740 unpack gives the order: live \, target \, value \, and then arg names the row \, fires the action and picks which row to draw. Two stores hold the scaled value because each row bangs its own (C-3)., f 84;
#X text 20 920 makefilename bpm \, with no % \, still takes the float and ignores it \, so a destination with no arg needs no special case. + 0.5 then i rounds \, so the tempo cannot flicker between 119.7 and 120.1 either., f 84;
#X obj 20 1100 inlet;
#X obj 20 1160 unpack f f f f;
